## A B S T R A C T
Highly selective ion separation is crucial to engineering applications. In this work, inspired by the working mechanism of biological protein ion channels, inclusion of ion-imprinted conductive polymers inside metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) with size selective effect was considered for the effective separation of ions. Insitu polymerization of polypyrrole (PPy) with anion exchange behavior in the UiO-66 cages was performed to obtain a conductive chlorine ion-imprinted polymer film electrode (Cl-IIP@UiO-66) for the electrochemically selective separation of chloride ions $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-})$ from wastewater. Herein, the chlorine ion-imprinted conductive PPy chains not only tuned the pore size of UiO-66, but also provided ion and electron transport channels at the molecular scale. As a result, the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion exchange capacity of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 coated electrode reached to $52.16\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with an adsorption equilibrium time less than $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ under the extra electric field. Meanwhile, the ClIIP $@$ UiO-66 film exhibited high separation factors of 9.02, 10.29, 13.41 and 18.50 for $\mathrm{{Cl}^{-}/B r^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{Cl^{-}/F^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{Cl^{-}/}$ $s0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Cl^{-}/P O_{4}^{3-}}$ , respectively, which was mainly attributed to the anion-imprinting effect and the nano­ structure for the selective recognition for $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions. Remarkably, based on the controlled swelling property of MOFs, the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 maintained superior electrochemical reversibility and durability over $98\%$ even after 1000 uptake/release cycles. Hence, it is believed that such a novel electroactive $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion-imprinted film electrode could be a promising candidate for wastewater treatment. Also, it opened a new pathway to develop novel MOFbased electroactive ion exchange materials for various ion separations.
## 1. Introduction
Selective ion separation always occurs in natural and biological systems, which inspires the researchers to develop novel techniques for scientific research and engineering applications, such as the purification of halide ions, separation of proteins, the reclamation of precious metals, and the removal of radioactive or heavy metal ions from wastewater [1–4]. Traditional organic adsorbents or ion exchange resins have limited selectivity due to the irregular free-spaces, low surface area and the swelling behavior [5]. Imitation of natural protein ionic gating platforms, tremendous efforts have been made to develop ordered porous materials such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent organic frameworks (COFs) and molecular sieves, for highly selective separation of ions through tunable angstrom-size pores based on variable ion channel gating mechanisms [6–8]. Nevertheless, owing to the lack of driving forces and the specific binding sites between target ions and functional groups, high ion diffusion resistance and secondary pollution generated by the adsorbent regeneration are still remained [9,10].
To enhance the ion separation efficiency, electrochemically switched ion exchange (ESIX) technology based on the electroactive ion exchange material (EIXM) has attracted great attention because of its high selec­ tivity, high adsorption capacity and no secondary pollution [11,12]. In the ESIX process, the external electric field is the main driving force for ion separation and the reversible uptake/release of target ions can be achieved just by controlling the redox state of the EIXM [13,14]. To date, the various EIXMs have been widely developed for the rapid and selective recovery of target cations such as $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ [15,16], ${\mathsf{C}}s^{+}$ [17], $\mathsf{C a^{2+}}$ [18], $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ [19], $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ [20], and $\mathrm{Y}^{3+}$ [21], as well as anions such as $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ [22], $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ [23], and $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ [24], from wastewater. The separation perfor­ mance of EIXM is generally related not only to the ionic size, electro­ static effect, and ion hydration force, but also to the traits of adsorbent structure such as nanopore structure, interlayer spacing and the specific interaction between target species and the binding site, which could contribute to the design of novel EIXMs to meet growing requirements for the purification and reclamation of multifarious target ions [25]. Chloride ion $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-})$ is a ubiquitous anion in wastewater discharged from livestock farms, leather industry, and landfill [26]. Excessive concen­ tration of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions always leads to the pollution of drinking water, salinization of soil, hindering of plants growth and corrosion of pipeline [27–29]. Nowadays, it is still full of challenge to efficiently and selec­ tively separate $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\left(6.64\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ from the wastewater with co-existing ions of the same valence or similar subnanometer-sized hydrated ion diameters such as $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ $(7.00\mathrm{~\AA})$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ (6.60 Å), $s0_{4}^{2-}$ (6.78 Å), and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ (7.50 Å) [30,31]. In fact, there is an obvious hydration enthalpy difference be­ tween the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}(-340\mathbf{\kJ\mol}^{-1})$ and other anion such as $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ $\left(-358\mathrm{kJ}\right.$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ), $\mathbf{F}^{-}\left(-580\mathbf{kJmol}^{-1}\right)$ , $s0_{4}^{2-}$ $(-1145\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ ( 2765 $\mathbf{kJmol}^{-1}.$ ) [32]. Therefore, it is possible to selectively separate $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ from the wastewater based on the dehydration mechanism if the ion separa­ tion film with an angstrom-scale pore architecture approaching the size of the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion could be constructed.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been considered as attrac­ tive building blocks to construct high-performance ion separation ma­ terials due to their tunable pore sizes, high surface areas and welldefined structures in the nanometer scale [33,34]. Zirconium-based MOF UiO-66 (UiO: University of Oslo) with an effective angstromsized aperture in diameter approaching the ion size of the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and exceptional stability against harsh conditions has recently proven to be a promising candidate for desalination and ion separation [35,36]. Espe­ cially, the triangular window sizes $({\sim}6.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ of UiO-66 are less than the hydrated ionic diameter of the above-mentioned anions but larger than their dehydrated ionic diameters such as $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ (2.66 Å), $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}\left(3.62\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ $(3.92\mathrm{~\AA~})$ , $s0_{4}^{2-}$ (4.60 Å), and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ $(4.76\mathrm{~\AA})$ (Table S1). Owing to the angstrom-scale confinement effect of UiO-66, any hydrated anions up­ take must undergo a dehydration process through the pore windows and thus need to consume energy, thereby increasing ion transfer resistance and slowing ion-exchange rate [37]. Fortunately, ESIX technique pro­ vides a tactic that an external electric field serves as an extra driving force to compensate the energy for dehydration of hydrated ions. However, UiO-66 has poor conductivity with the absence of ion ex­ change sites, which could not meet demands of EIXM with both elec­ tronic and ionic conductivities.
Molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs) based on molecularly imprinted techniques (MITs) are a class of artificially synthesized smart polymer materials, where a large number of imprinted cavities com­ plementary to the template molecules in shape, size, and functionality and designed for the selective recognition and separation of target species are distributed [38,39]. Cheng et al. [40] synthesized a molec­ ularly imprinted electrochemical sensor based on biomass carbon decorated with MOF-derived $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and silver nanoparticles for selective and sensitive detection of nitrofurazone. Similarly, ion-imprinted poly­ mers (IIPs) were developed based on MIPs and retained all the advan­ tages of MIPs, but they can specifically recognize ions [41]. Polypyrrole (PPy) is one of the typical conducting polymers with good conductivity and electrochemical activity in neutral solution [42,43]. It is found that its cation exchange behavior can be achieved by doping large immobile anions such as polystyrenesulfonate $(\mathrm{PSS^{n-}})$ [44]. While, its anion ex­ change behavior can be endowed by doping small and mobile anions such as $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ due to the high mobility of these ions in the polymer backbone [45]. Weidlich et al. [46] considered that the type, size, valence state of the counterion during the synthesis process and the ions in the different electrolyte solution could affect the ion exchange properties of PPy. Kong et al. [47] synthesized a $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ doped PPy (PPy/ $\mathbf{Cl}^{-\cdot}$ ) electrode for the chloride-storage in seawater desalination. In our previous study, an iodine trapping polypyrrole $(\mathrm{PPy/I^{-}})$ electroactive film was obtained, which exhibited a high adsorption capacity with superior selectivity to $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ion owing to the special $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ion memory effect [24]. Nevertheless, PPy suffers from poor cyclic stability since the vol­ ume expansion and shrinkage during the ion extraction/insertion pro­ cess result in the cracking and breaking, thereby leading to the loss of ion-exchange sites [48]. To solve this issue, herein, PPy could be grown in the UiO-66 cavities to achieve a win–win situation through a synergy effect. On the one hand, the disordered conductive PPy chains as the electronic bridge could be separated with the framework of UiO-66, where the ion-exchange sites on the PPy backbone can be fully exposed to the surface, which would provide electron and ion transport path­ ways. On the other hand, the narrow aperture in the UiO-66 could serve as ion sieving channels for complementary separation of ions selectively whereas its nanometer-sized cavities might constrain the swelling of PPy in an extending conformation to enhance the electrochemical durability. In addition, the diversity of MOFs and polymers should be beneficious for the essentially possible combination [49]. This is similar the work of Kitagawa et al. [50], in which they developed an attractive strategy to introduce poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) (PEDOT) inside the MIL101(Cr) by in-situ polymerization of adsorbed monomers in the nano­ channels, resulting in composites possessing both electronic conduc­ tivity and high porosity.
In this study, PPy as $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion imprinted polymer (Cl-IIP) was threaded into UiO-66 to prepare a composite film coated electrode (noted as Cl$\scriptstyle{\mathrm{IIP}@\mathrm{UiO}-66}\$ by a confined polymerization strategy with a further ion de-doping step. To the best of our knowledge, the inclusion of a conductive ion-imprinted polymer in a MOF for selective separation of halide ions has not been reported, especially in an ESIX system. Owing to the nano-confinement effect of UiO-66 and the assistance of ion imprinting mechanism, the obtained Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated elec­ trode is expected to have excellent electrochemical performance, mak­ ing it a promising candidate for the capture and separation of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions in the practical wastewater treatment process.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Reagents and instruments
Pyrrole (Sigma-Aldrich) was distilled under reduced pressure and stored in the nitrogen atmosphere until use. Zirconium (IV) chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4}}$ , Sigma-Aldrich, $98\%$ , terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C}$ , Sigma-Aldrich, $98\%$ , and N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) were bought from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Other chemicals were analytical grade and purchased from National Medicine Group Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). Aqueous solutions were prepared by using Millipore deionized water $(18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm})$ . The titanium mesh $(2\times1.5\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ was pretreated by soaking successively in $5\%$ sodium carbonate solution and $10\%$ oxalic acid solution at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ , and then washing by water and drying before use.
## 2.2. Material synthesis
The metal-organic framework UiO-66 was prepared by a hydro­ thermal method following the procedure in the reported literature [35]. Firstly, $0.3689g$ of zirconium chloride, $0.2604{\mathrm g}$ of terephthalic acid and $28\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of deionized water were sealed in a Teflon-lined autoclave with 60 mL of DMF and heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, the autoclave was cooled down to room temperature and the obtained white powder was repeatedly washed by ethanol to remove the unreacted reagents in the pores with the assistance of centrifugation. Finally, the UiO-66 powder was dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum condition.
A conducting $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion-imprinted polymer (Cl-IIP) was synthesized by a simple chemical oxidative method. $0.8\mathrm{M}\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ aqueous solution was added to $0.2\mathrm{~M~}$ pyrrole in an ice bath under constant stirring for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The obtained black precipitate was washed with deionized water and methanol successively for three times to remove the residual $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ and oligomers, and finally dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . For com­ parison, a conducting non-imprinted polymer (NIP) was prepared in the similar manner through $s0_{4}^{2-}$ ion as ion dopant but without template $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion in the electrolyte to investigate the recognition ability of Cl-IIP [51].
Pristine UiO-66 ( $\mathrm{150mg}$ ) was immersed into a glass vial with $2\mathrm{mL}$ of pyrrole solution for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere to make the pyrrole monomer molecules entering UiO-66 nanopores $(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{Py})$ . Then, after UiO- $66@$ Py was put in the vacuum oven at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to remove external redundant Py monomers from UiO-66, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.8\mathbf{M}$ $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ solution was added and maintained in an ice bath for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the black Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 precipitate after the oxidation polymerization was washed with deionized water and methanol successively for three times, and dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of Cl-IIP@UiO-66 film coated electrode
Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode was prepared as follows. The obtained Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 powder $(80\mathrm{wt\%})$ as the active material, carbon black $(10~\mathrm{wt\%})$ as the conducting agent and poly (vinylidene fluoride) $(10~\mathrm{wt}^{\dag})\dag$ ) as a binder were mixed in N-methyl pyrrolidinone (NMP)
solution with stirring at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to form a slurry, which was coated on the pretreated titanium mesh $(2\ \times\ 1.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ as the working electrode. Herein, the mass coating amount of the active ma­ terial was about $30.0\pm\mathrm{~3~mg}$ . Then, the electrode was dried under vacuum at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Prior to the electrochemical performance test, the imprinted $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions were in situ removed at a constant voltage of $-0.8\mathrm{V}$ to get $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion vacancies on the electrode. For comparison, the ClIIP, NIP and UiO-66 were also separately coated on pretreated titanium mesh under the same conditions.
## 2.4. Electrochemical performance
Electrochemical performance was tested in a three-electrode system with the prepared electroactive film coated electrode as the working electrode, and a platinum electrode and a $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ electrode as the counter and reference electrodes, respectively, under a constant poten­ tial of $0.8\mathrm{V}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ with $10\mathrm{mL}$ of KCl aqueous solution at different initial concentrations of 50, 100, 200 and $300\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured with an open circuit voltage of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ in a frequency range of $0.01\ \mathrm{\Hz}\mathrm{-}100\ \mathrm{\kHz}$ . The adsorption capacity $(\mathsf{Q})$ of the electrode was calculated using the following equation:
$$
Q=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})\times V}{m}
$$
where $\mathbf{C}_{0}~(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}~\mathbf{L}^{-1})$ and ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}})$ are the initial and equilibrium concentrations of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions, respectively; V (mL) is the volume of the aqueous solution; and m (g) is the mass of the active material on the electrode. In addition, ion exchange (IX) performance of the electrode without extra electric field was also tested. In order to investigate the selectivity of the Cl-IIP@UiO-66 film coated electrode for ${\mathrm{Cl}}^{-}/{\mathrm{F}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/$ $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}$ , the selective adsorption experiment was carried out in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixed solution containing $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , $s0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ with each concentration of $100\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The selectivity of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film electrode for $\mathrm{Cl^{-}/F^{-}}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}/\mathbf{Br}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}/$ $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ was tested by using competitive adsorption in a mixed solution of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ , $s0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ with each concentration of $100\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ by applying a constant potential of $_{0.8\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . For comparison, the electrodes with the UiO-66 and Cl-IIP films were also investigated under the same condition. The distribution coefficients $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{D}}~(\mathrm{L}~g^{-1})$ and the relative separation factor $\upalpha_{\mathrm{{M}}}^{\mathrm{{Cl}^{-}}}$ was calculated using the following equations, respectively.
$$
K_{D}={\frac{C_{0}-C_{e}}{C_{e}}}\times{\frac{V}{m}}
$$
$$
\alpha_{M}^{C l^{-}}\ =\ \frac{K_{D}(C l^{-})}{K_{D}(M)}
$$
where ${\bf C}_{0}$ $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}_{0}\ (\mathrm{mg\L^{-1}})$ and ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}\mathrm{\overline{{{\tau}}}}$ ) are the initial and equilibrium concentrations of corresponding anion concentrations of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{{\bfF}}^{-}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{r}}^{-}$ $s0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ , respectively; V (mL) is the volume of the aqueous so­ lution; m $\mathbf{\delta}(\mathbf{g})$ is the mass of the active material on the electrode; and $\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{D}}(\mathsf{C l}^{-})$ and ${\mathrm{K}}_{\mathrm{D}}({\mathrm{M}})$ represent the distribution coefficients of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and other anions, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations
Fig. 1a shows XRD patterns of UiO-66, Cl-IIP and Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66. Herein, the spectrum of pristine UiO-66 was matched well with the simulated UiO-66 and the characteristic diffraction peaks of UiO-66 structure were the same as those reported in the literature [52]. In contrast, the pattern of the Cl-IIP showed a broad peak in the region of $20{-}30^{\circ}$ , which was attributed to the fact that the polymer was basically amorphous [53]. It should be noted that the spectrum of Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 was consistent with that of the prepared UiO-66, indicating maintenance of the crystal structure of UiO-66 after the polymerization, which manifested that the loading of Cl-IIP did not change the parent crystal structure of UiO-66. The presence of Cl-IIP in UiO-66 was further confirmed by the FT-IR measurement. As shown in Fig. 1b, in the spectrum of UiO-66, the characteristic peak at $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the stretching vibrations of $\mathtt{C=}0$ in the carboxylic acid present in $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ [54]. The representative vibrations peaks at 1400 and 1588 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ associated with the carboxylate groups of the $\mathrm{\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}}$ ligand and the peak at $746~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ were also observed [55]. While the small band at $1508~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ represented the typical frame vi­ bration of a benzene ring [56]. For the Cl-IIP, the absorption peaks observed at $1540~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1459~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belonged to the symmetric and asymmetric pyrrole ring stretching vibration of $\mathrm{PPy}$ , respectively [53]. The sharp band at around $1396~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was linked to the in-plane defor­ mation of the ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\tt H}$ bond. The band at $1295~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was ascribed to the $\mathtt{=c-H}$ in-plane stretching vibration and the band at $1033~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ cor­ responded to the ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\tt H}$ mode deformation vibration [42]. The typical characteristic peak at $1157~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the stretching $\mathsf{C-N}$ mode of vibration [57]. The characteristic peak at $966\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belonged to the stretching vibration of doped PPy [58]. The absorption bands around $896~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $779\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the out of plane vibrations of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ mode [59]. Both the characteristic bands of UiO-66 and Cl-IIP were found in the spectrum of Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66. Interestingly, one can see that some characteristic bands of Cl-IIP appeared blue shifts, which were attributed to the fact that the Cl-IIP chains formed in the confined UiO-66 template were oriented, which increased the conjugate length of the polymer, and thus constrained the vibration of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bond [60,61]. Such obvious blue shifts also indicated the occurrence of interaction between Cl-IIP chains and the MOF skeletons, which is further demonstrated by the XPS results.
As shown in Fig. 2a, the survey XPS spectrum confirms that the Cl$\mathrm{IIP}@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ mainly contained C, O, Zr, N, and Cl elements, which is in good agreement with the TEM-EDS spectrum and elemental mapping analysis (Fig. S2). Fig. 2b shows the high-resolution XPS core level spectrum of $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}$ . The binding energy peaks locating at $183.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 185.4 eV corresponding to $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}~3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ respectively were observed [62], indicating the presence of Zr (VI) for the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ -cluster in ClIIP@UiO-66, which is the representative structure of UiO-66. For com­ parison, the high-resolution XPS spectra of pure UiO-66 was also tested, where the $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ spectrum was deconvoluted into two peaks at $182.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $185.2\mathrm{eV}$ assigned to Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively [63]. It is worth noting that the binding energies of $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}$ for $\mathrm{Cl-IIP}@\mathrm{UiO-}66$ shifted positively towards high binding energies compared to the pure UiO-66, which could be rationally ascribed to the interaction between the UiO-66 and Cl-IIP [62]. Fig. 2c shows that the binding energy peaks at 197.2, 198.4, and $200.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ appeared in the XPS spectrum of the composite film corresponded to the Cl 2p [47]. The Cl $\mathsf{2p2/1}$ at $198.4\mathrm{eV}$ was related to the charge-transfer interaction between the chloride ions and the polypyrrole chain [64]. The Cl $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ peaks at 197.2 and 200.7 eV are related to the presence of ionic and covalent chlorides [64], which confirmed that $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions as template species can be doped in the composite. Also, as shown in Fig. 2d, the N 1s peak of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 can be fitted with into four Gaussian peaks at binding energies of 397.6, 399.7, 400.6 and $402.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are assigned to nitrogen species in imine nitrogen $\scriptstyle(-\mathbf{N}=)$ , neutral amine nitrogen (–NH–), polaron $(\mathrm{-NH^{+}-})$ and bipolaron $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{N}^{+})$ ), respectively [21,47]. These results obviously indicate that the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 was successfully fabricated.
Fig. 3 shows SEM and HRTEM images of UiO-66 and Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 composite. As shown in Fig. 3a, the obtained UiO-66 crystals were well intergrown with sharp edges and displayed a uniform morphology with particle sizes of $\sim100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Meanwhile, the Cl-IIP exhibited a typical cauliflower-like morphology (Fig. S1) as reported in the literature [45]. One can see in Fig. 3d and that the morphology of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 had no obvious change from original one and agglomerated Cl-IIP was not observed on the surface of the composite after the polymerization. HRTEM images showed successful inclusion of Cl-IIP inside the voids of the framework. As is shown by the yellow arrows in Fig. 3f, nanofiberlike structures with a dark contrast were observed in Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66, indicating ion-imprinted polymer chains were successfully grown in­ side the voids of the framework. Similar results have been observed in other reports [55,65]. Besides, the EDS mapping via HRTEM in Fig. S2 indicated uniform distributions of the elements N and Cl, revealing a uniform distribution of Cl-IIP into the UiO-66 framework.
$\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm at 77 K and the pore size distribution analysis were investigated. As shown in Fig. 4a, both the curves for the UiO-66 and Cl-IIP@UiO-66 exhibited the typical I-type isotherms, indicative of the microporous nature, and the curve of Cl-IIP was agreed with the IItype isotherm for the nonporous material. A dramatic decrease in BET surface area from $1094.65\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for pristine UiO-66 to $417.78\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 was observed, indicating that the chlorine ionimprinted polymers did not completely cover the external surface of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 and block pore access, but threaded into the frame­ work cavities of UiO-66 without completely filling them [66]. Herein, if the guest polymer molecules only covered the outside surface of UiO-66, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption amount of Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 should be much lower than this value [66,67]. Meanwhile, as shown in Table S2, the pore volume of UiO-66 was decreased from $0.56\ g\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to $0.33\mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ , which should be ascribed to the cavities of UiO-66 were partly filled with Cl-IIP chains. The pore size distribution curves determined by using Horvath-Kawazoe (HK) model with cylindrical geometry further confirmed this conjecture. Fig. 4b showed a narrow peak at about $6.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and a broad peak at $9.{\overset{-}{0}}-11.0{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}}$ , which were close to the pore sizes estimated from the crystal structure of UiO-66 aperture and cavities, respectively [52]. As such, the cavities of UiO-66 should be large enough to accommodate pyrrole molecules $(4.3\times3.7\mathring{\mathbb{A}}^{2})$ for easily impregnating into the nanopores (Fig. S3). As expected, with the polymerization of pyrrole in the crystal structure of UiO-66, the aperture of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 decreased from 6.5 to $6.3{\mathring\mathrm{A}}.$ . In addition, it should be noted that the broad peak at around $9.0{-}11.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ corresponding to the cavities of UiO-66 almost disappeared. These observations also indicated that the majority of Cl-IIP chains should be formed in the cavities through the entrances of UiO-66 [55]. Moreover, such an apparent decrease in pore size distribution also implied that the polymer might be formed near the side pore walls, leaving the remaining space unimpeded, which could sufficiently expose more ion-imprinted sites (Fig. S3).
TGA curve was used to evaluated how much of the Cl-IIP was caged in the framework of UiO-66. As shown in Fig. 5, there were three decomposition stages in the TGA curve of the UiO-66 sample. The first stage from 25 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ corresponded to the desorption of the adsorbed water molecules [68]. The second stage decreased from 100 to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ indicated the degradation of solvent DMF in the material [68]. In the third range of $480-565\ {}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},$ the obvious descending process should be resulted from the decomposition of organic benzene ring and pore collapse of UiO-66 material structure, and the final residue was mainly attributed to the $\mathrm{ZrO_{2}}$ with low crystallinity [52]. Whereas the signifi­ cant weight loss of the Cl-IIP starts at about $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which corresponded to the degradation of polymer [69]. Besides, the TGA curve for the Cl$\mathrm{IIP}@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ was similar to that of UiO-66. As such, the calculated weight percentage of Cl-IIP in the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 was as high as $53.14\%$ .
## 3.2. Electrochemically switched ion exchange (ESIX) mechanism
A feasible schematic illustration for the reversible uptake/release of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ by the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 electroactive film based on size effect and ion imprinting mechanism is shown in Fig. 6. The corresponding electro­ chemical adsorption/desorption processes can be expressed according to the following reaction:
$$
\mathrm{PPy^{+}{\cdot}C l^{-}+e^{-}\Leftarrow P P y+C l^{-}}
$$
Herein, the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 electroactive film can be oxidized/pro­ tonated by applied an oxidation potential, resulting in a positively charged PPy backbone. In this case, the hydrated $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions will undergo a partial dehydration process to pass through the cavities due to the size confinement effect of MOFs, which follows a similar dehydration mechanism occurred in the biological ion channels [70], and the dehydrated $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions are incorporated to the imprinted sites with the assistance of an external electric field to maintain the charge balance within the polymer. Meanwhile, the strong interaction between the imprinted ion and the binding site on the PPy backbone becomes an internal driving force for the target ion transferring from one site to another. Such a dual driving force can compensate the consumed hy­ dration energy. On the contrary, when a reduction potential is applied on the Cl-IIP@UiO-66 film, the nitrogen atoms in PPy can be reduced and thus the positive charge of PPy disappears, which forces the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ out of the backbone and into the solution.
To further identify the ESIX mechanism, Fig. 7 shows the highresolution XPS spectra of the N and Cl region for the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 electroactive film treated by different redox states. As shown in Fig. 7a, it is worth noting that the peak of polaron $(\mathrm{-NH^{+}-})$ and bipolaron $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{N}^{+})$ significantly weakened under a reduction potential of $-0.8\mathrm{V}$ applying to the film and the adsorbed $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions could be flushed out from the composite film to balance the positive charge of polaron in PPy. As expected, the Cl 2p peaks corresponding to several chlorine species almost disappeared after a reduction voltage of $-0.8\mathrm{V}$ is applied to the film electrode. Meanwhile, Fig. 7b shows the binding energy peaks at 197.2, 198.4, and $200.6\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the Cl 2p appeared in the XPS spectrum of the composite film, which confirmed that $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions can be inserted into the composite film at an oxidation potential of $0.8{\mathrm{~V}}{}$ , which shows a good agreement with EDS result as shown in Fig. S4. This result indicated that the electrochemically controlled up­ take/release of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions can be realized by adjusting the redox state of the composite film.
## 3.3. ESIX characterization of the Cl-IIP@UiO-66 film coated electrod
The electrochemical behavior of Cl-IIP@UiO-66 film coated elec­ trode was investigated in a three-electrode system. Firstly, electro­ chemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured to understand the ion diffusion and charge transfer resistances of the electrode/electrolyte interface. As shown in Fig. 8a, the semicircular diameter of the Nyquist impedance spectrum of UiO-66 was evidently larger than that of the ClIIP, indicating that the UiO-66 has a large charge transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ . Additionally, the $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of Cl-IIP was less than that of NIP, confirming that the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ imprinting cavities generated with the removal of the in-situ doping $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion provide ion vacancies for the target ions to go through the redox reaction. Compared with the Cl-IIP, the smaller Rct value of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode indicated a higher ionic conductivity with a lower electron transfer resistance. Herein, the UiO-66 should increase the conjugate delocalization length of PPy and enhance the charge-carrier transport, which is consistent with the FT-IR analysis result. This indicated that Cl-IIP with both ionic and electronic conductivity confined into UiO-66 can improve the conductivity of whole composites. Meanwhile, the ultra-hydrophilicity of UiO-66 (Fig. S5) made the surface of Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film more easily get wetted, which could also promote electrolyte ions to enter the inner pores of Cl-IIP@UiO-66. In addition, one can see that the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode presented a steeper line than the Cl-IIP film coated electrode at the lower frequency, indicating a lower diffusion resistance behavior [71].
Fig. 8b shows cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode at different scan rates of 25, 50, 100, 200 and 400 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}\thinspace s^{-1}$ in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KCl solution, in which the characteristic oxidation and reduction peaks at about $0.8\mathrm{V}$ and $-0.8\mathrm{V}$ , respectively were observed. One can see that the oxidation and reduction peaks were highly sym­ metrical, indicating that the electrochemical reaction on this electrode should be reversible. As shown in the right inset of Fig. 8b, both the oxidation and reduction peak currents with the square root of the scan rate $(\mathbf{v}^{1/2})$ maintained a linear relationship with the correlation coeffi­ cient of 0.9998, indicating that the electron transfer on the electrode surface should be a diffusion-controlling process [14].
## 3.4. Electrochemical adsorption and desorption of the $C l^{-}$ ions
Fig. 9a shows the effect of initial concentration on the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion adsorption performance of the $\mathrm{Cl-IIP}@\mathrm{UiO-}66$ film coated electrode. With the increase of initial concentration, the adsorption quantity increased, which was derived from the stronger driving force at a higher initial concentration of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions. In particular, when the initial con­ centration of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions was $300\mathrm{~mg~L}^{-1}$ , the adsorption capacity for $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions reached to $52.16\ \mathrm{mg\8^{-1}}$ , which was a relatively high value in comparison with other reported $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion adsorbents (Table S3). As stated above, such a high adsorption capacity should be ascribed to the syn­ ergistic effect of the full exposure of more ion-imprinted sites in the highly porous UiO-66 and the extra electric driving force in the ion transfer. However, it is found that the removal percentage of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions in the solutions with either 200 or $300\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ ions decreased greatly (Fig. S6) since this electrode had limited saturation adsorption capacity [72].
In addition, the kinetics of adsorption were further investigated to understand the adsorption rate and the potential rate controlling step in the ESIX process by using he pseudo-first-order model (5) as well as the pseudo-second-order model (6) as expressed in the follows:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\displaystyle\ln({\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}-{\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}})=\ln{\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}-{\bf k}_{1}\mathrm{t}}}}\\ {{{\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{t}}{{\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}}=\frac{\mathrm{t}}{{\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}}+\frac{1}{{\bf k}_{2}{\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}}}}\end{array}
$$
To estimate the long-term stability of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode, the change in the charging capacity was calculated by the integrated charges at $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ for 1000 cycles in $300\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ ion solution. As shown in Fig. 11, after 1000 cycles, the charging capacity of the Cl-IIP film coated electrode gradually decreased to about $42\%$ of its initial value. Herein, the poor cyclic stability was mainly resulted from the swelling and shrinkage of PPy during the redox process, which led to the deterioration of the film. In comparison, the charging capacity of the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode gradually increased to some extent during the first 200 cycles, which may be attributed to the fact that the ion transfer resistance decreased with the ion uptake/release so that more $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion binding sites were activated at the beginning. Thereafter, the charging capacity remained more than $98\%$ of its initial value even after 1000 cycles. The structure of composite film electrode was not changed significantly according to the XRD spectrum after $1000~\mathrm{CV}$ cycles as shown in Fig. S10, indicating that the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode exhibited superior electrochemical stability. Herein, as stated above, the nanometer-sized cavities of UiO-66 provided confined and narrow spaces for volume changes of PPy during the repeatedly ion uptake/release process, which effectively alleviated the volumetric stress and irreversible degradation so that the durability of the PPy was improved.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the chlorine ion-imprinted conductive polymer was successfully caged into the MOF cavities to obtained a high-performance
Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 based electrode for the selective separation of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions from wastewater using an ESIX process for the first time. Attributed to the size effect and the specific imprinted ion vacancies derived from ion memory mechanism, the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode showed outstanding selectivity towards $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions with a $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion exchange ca­ pacity as high as $52.16\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in the aqueous solution with $300\mathrm{\mg{L}}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions. Furthermore, with the effective confinement effect of UiO-66, the Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode retained high electrochemical stability even after 1000 uptake/release cycles. It is expected that such an outstanding Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film coated electrode can be used as a promising electroactive material for efficient removal of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ion from waste water. Moreover, the successful synthesis of Cl-IIP $@$ UiO-66 film electrode not only simultaneously constructs three-dimensional porous ion and electron transport channels at the molecular level to enhance the electrical conductivity of MOFs but also may open up a new pathway to design novel MOF-based electroactive ion exchange materials with su­ perior electrochemical performance for the potential applications in the wastewater treatment.
